{:edb/db-before-t 2446 :edb/db-after-t 2447 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535759 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:40:58.235Z" 13194139535759 true] [17592186156929 1100 "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(\\pi,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/08502aa81729d4f88aec094e2e2f5c7b4a57825ef3767f1eec29700ceb6e21a3.png)" 13194139535759 true] [17592186156929 1100 "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(\\pi,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{y}\\cos x & e^{y}\\sin x\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535759 false] [17592186156929 1145 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{y}\\cos x & e^{y}\\sin x\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = e^y\\cos x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =e^y\\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{y}\\cos x) =-e^{y}\\sin x & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{y}\\sin x) = e^{y}\\cos x \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{y}\\cos x) = e^{y}\\cos x & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{y}\\sin x) = e^{y}\\sin x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-e^{y}\\sin x & e^{y}\\cos x \\\\ e^{y}\\cos x & e^{y}\\sin x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(\\pi,0)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535759 false] [17592186156929 1145 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/025dd1a0b89b52e32a3e42e5bd3492261ed060f104f627051b6950c7f94d66c1.png)\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/f1a47569220f01e1dc474a13a5040e969449ee0b10cfe7df39c3aec4c6175f41.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/f0b46d26ccc6fefc404b021bde11c4d6959c00ae0ef1145a9984da602bcc54ef.png)\n\nFirst, notice that since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/964f8ac9ca9c305faef8562f66d063f318ecbe402df337377bd9efdf61301567.png) \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = e^y\\cos x ,  \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =e^y\\sin x .\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(\\pi,0).$ This gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/540c920abd2989b29aae275eedd179c5118e82354915e591c0d1c5296ca9fa21.png)" 13194139535759 true] [17592186156930 1100 "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix}$\nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1)$." 13194139535759 false] [17592186156930 1100 "Given that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/b3e96b1a54af842b69da4ae5fcb44858bffdac48b27cde6677c15c05906e890d.png) \n evaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" 13194139535759 true] [17592186156930 1145 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det \\left(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\ln y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac xy\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln y) = 0 & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) = \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln y) = \\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) =-\\frac{x}{y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{″}(0, 1) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1) = 0 \\cdot 0 - 1\\cdot 1 = -1\n$$" 13194139535759 false] [17592186156930 1145 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/af5524049f5f8b3c4c4241520f2d874b454d61b1b025362f5193437f766e610b.png)\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/8882516e2732e419efdfb8439265212dd4f380d6a6ea58f6ff1291030bf50bfb.png)\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det \\left( \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/4fede2bb5c2306f62b55b7996488baee52c69e7d41b011e0e9d2b7405e63a8f4.png)\n\nFirst, notice that since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/9252d1ce4f5df828f45351d01e0709cf2f2ca5df2c9c25390c111f40ca00fb22.png) we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\ln y, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1) = 0 \\cdot 0 - 1\\cdot 1 = -1.\n$$" 13194139535759 true] [17592186156931 1100 "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)  = x^2 e^y.$" 13194139535759 true] [17592186156931 1100 "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y) = x^2 e^y$." 13194139535759 false] [17592186156931 1145 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}e^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}e^{y})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2xe^{y} & x^{2}e^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xe^{y}) = 2e^{y} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}e^{y}) = 2xe^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xe^{y}) = 2xe^{y} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}e^{y}) = x^{2}e^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}2e^{y} & 2xe^{y} \\\\ 2xe^{y} & x^{2}e^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at\n\n$$\n\\left(1,0\\right)\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nf^{″}(1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535759 false] [17592186156931 1145 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/7444a8128c085d7bf4f56a57a60c260864a50da0b744b6dbe22a2ced89a0588d.png)\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/22dd960829ea268e5aade685b56949f8998f87ae8c040a90f04436cbfc6b9a58.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/4fede2bb5c2306f62b55b7996488baee52c69e7d41b011e0e9d2b7405e63a8f4.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin